## 2. Predicted-outcome balance row (TODO at 6.2, line 511; R7 item A3)

**Question.** Convert whatever imbalance exists on observed pre-exposure traits into the reduced-form bias it implies, so "detect no such group at the precision available" becomes a number read against 0.02039.

**Sample.** S0, the headline exposure rows (2,682,330).

**Construction.** As specified in R7 A1–A3, restated:
1. On S0 with the fixed effects and X0-NH (the headline controls minus the recipient-history and volume controls), regress the later-grant outcome Y on the fifteen traits T15 entered jointly, each standardised to unit SD on the estimation rows. Keep the fifteen coefficients.
2. Form Ŷ_i = Σ_k b_k · trait_ik (traits only; no fixed effects, no X0-NH).
3. Regress Ŷ on the pooled score under the Panel A model (fixed effects, X0-NH, score in reference-SD units of 0.0594), shared-account-or-date covariance.
4. Repeat 1–3 with the receipt indicator D in place of Y to get the D̂ row.

**Report.** Coefficient and SE of step 3, in percentage points per reference SD; the same for D̂. Also the R² of step 1 (how much of Y the traits predict), since the bias row is only as informative as that fit.

**Read.** The Ŷ coefficient is the implied reduced-form bias from imbalance on observed traits. Against the reduced form 0.02039 (SE 0.009): a value below about 0.002 means observed-trait imbalance can account for at most a tenth of the reduced form; a value near 0.02 means the balance evidence does not separate the two. The D̂ row is read against the first stage 6.912.

**Lands.** A row in Table 5 Panel B beneath the single-trait line ("Implied reduced-form bias from observed traits"), and the 6.2 sentence rewritten as "The recipients at the margin of entry do not differ on the traits that predict the outcome: the imbalance on all fifteen observed traits implies a reduced-form bias of [x] points (SE [s]), against a reduced form of 0.020." The A4(c) version on the F50 entry frame is the natural companion and can replace "at the precision available" in the entry paragraph.

