## 3. Which account of the board: three analyses (TODO at 7.2, line 536)

The three share one purpose: desert says the chooser responds to the opponent's performance on the board; expectations-based reference dependence says the chooser responds to their own winning chances. The two coincide on the evaluation gradient and differ on what else should matter.

### 3(1). Winning chances held fixed

**Training sample.** Standard rated pool games from the same 24 months that ended by checkmate, resignation, stalemate, agreed draw, repetition, fifty-move rule, or insufficient material. Exclude both timeout statuses (`outoftime` cannot be separated into disconnections and ordinary flag falls) and aborted games. Draw 2,000,000 games, stratified by speed category in the proportions of the analysis sample (Table A.2 Panel B).

**Positions.** One position per game. Sample the ply from the analysis sample's ply distribution within speed (so that the model is fitted where opportunities occur), restricted to plies strictly before the game's final ply. Evaluate it with the same engine and budget, from the side to move's perspective, matching the opportunity convention in which the disconnected player is the side to move. Record both clocks at that ply from the PGN `%clk` comments, both displayed ratings, the time-control base and increment, the speed category, and the ply.

**Model.** A cross-fitted multinomial model (five folds by game; gradient boosting or a flexible logit with splines in evaluation, ply, and both clocks, interacted with speed) of the game's result from the perspective of the side not to move, the chooser's analogue. Output the chooser-analogue's expected score Ê = P(win) + ½·P(draw). Report out-of-fold calibration by decile of Ê and by evaluation band.

**Apply.** Score every opportunity in the primary gradient sample (11,590,459 competitive opportunities after ply ten) with Ê from its final position, last recorded clocks, ratings, time control, speed, and ply. Report the distribution of Ê within the eight evaluation bins of Table B.1 Panel B, to show how much variation in Ê exists at fixed evaluation; that variation (from clocks, ratings, and ply) is the identifying variation.

**Regressions.** Three models on identical rows, the primary gradient model throughout:
- (i) evaluation spline only (reproduces Table B.1 row 1: 0.241 / 0.529 / 1.021);
- (ii) Ê only, as a natural cubic spline with knots at its quintiles; report the adjusted grant rate by decile of Ê;
- (iii) both. Report the three evaluation contrasts at fixed Ê and the Ê decile profile at fixed evaluation.

**Read.** Under reference dependence, the evaluation contrasts in (iii) collapse toward zero and the Ê profile survives. Under desert, the evaluation contrasts survive and the Ê profile flattens. Both surviving means the accounts are not separated at this resolution, which 7.2 then says with numbers.

**Cost.** 2,000,000 new engine evaluations; everything else is regression on existing data.

### 3(2). The chooser's own clock, by speed

**Sample and model.** The primary gradient sample and model with the clock controls of Table B.1 row 2, replacing the pooled log-seconds terms with categories of each player's last recorded remaining time as a share of the time-control base (below 10 percent; 10–25; 25–50; 50–100; above 100), with a missing category, each interacted with speed category (ultrabullet and bullet; blitz; rapid; classical).

**Report.** The chooser-clock category coefficients by speed (reference: 50–100 percent), the opponent-clock coefficients by speed, and the three evaluation contrasts, in one table. Add a row with the chooser's clock entered linearly as log seconds by speed for a one-number summary.

**Read.** Under reference dependence the chooser grants more as their own time falls, because their winning chances fall, and most in the fast speeds; under desert the chooser's own clock is irrelevant at a fixed board. The opponent's clock can matter under both.

**Note.** The last recorded reading is at the chooser's last move, before the countdown; say so in the note.

### 3(3). How the advantage was reached

**Sample.** The replay sample of Appendix B.2: 20,000 chooser accounts, 260,863 opportunities, every position before the final one evaluated with the last exact score; the 72,573 evaluated competitive opportunities after ply ten and the 213,388 pooled competitive-and-clearly-worse rows.

**Construction.** For each opportunity, per-ply evaluation changes from the rival's perspective, clipped at ±1,000 centipawns; mates as in B.2. On the rival's own moves the change is a non-positive loss; on the chooser's moves it is a non-negative gain for the rival. Define R = the rival's cumulative centipawn loss over its own moves and C = the chooser's cumulative loss over its own moves; the final evaluation A satisfies A ≈ C − R, so at a fixed endpoint the decomposition has one free dimension. Also define each player's average centipawn loss per move (ACPL = R or C divided by own moves), which is the platform's own accuracy measure and does not scale with game length.

**Model.** The B.2 specification (chooser, stage-band, speed, and month fixed effects; eight final-evaluation categories in the competitive region, an unrestricted spline in the clearly-worse region), adding the rival's ACPL (primary) or R per 100 centipawns (secondary), one at a time; then the pooled asymmetry model with the rival term interacted with the competitive region. Do not enter C alongside R in the totals model; it is implied by A and R.

**Report.** Rival-ACPL and R coefficients in each region, the interaction, the three planned contrasts with and without the term, and (as a bridge to the existing result) the last-move cost coefficient of Table B.2 re-estimated with the whole-game term present.

**Read.** At a fixed final position, a larger R means the rival's advantage was reached in a sloppier game: the rival gave more back and the chooser gave more away. Desert predicts a negative coefficient in the competitive region, since an advantage reached cleanly is better earned, and nothing in the clearly-worse region. Reference dependence predicts zero in both, since the endpoint is held fixed. The last-move cost of −0.181 per 100 centipawns (Table B.2) is the one-move version of this test.

**Lands (all three).** A new Section 4.6 "Winning chances and the opponent's play" of two paragraphs, three tables in Appendix B (B.4–B.6), and the 7.2 paragraph "Which account of the board" rewritten around the numbers. The TODO's claim "under reference dependence only (1) should matter; under desert, (3) should" becomes the paragraph's organising sentence, with (2) as the clock-side check.

